ng appears to be **B: f1-b5** because:\n- It develops a piece with tempo\n- Attacks the knight on c6\n- Black's King on d7 is in an awkward position, and this bishop move puts additional pressure\n- It's an aggressive move fitting my need to create complications given the material deficit\n\nReason: With a significant material disadvantage (Queen down), I cannot play passively. B: f1-b5 develops actively, attacks Black's knight, and exploits Black's King being on d7 rather than safely castled. This creates immediate tactical pressure and forces Black to respond to threats rather than consolidating their advantage.\n\nAnswer: B: f1-b5"}
