Black's king on e6 is critically exposed in the center of the board. While developing with N: g1-f3 is tempting, I should maintain maximum pressure on Black's awkward position. The bishop on b5 is already well-placed, and moving it to c6 not only attacks the knight but also controls critical central squares around Black's exposed king. However, the most forcing move is **N: g1-f3**, which develops my knight with tempo, prepares castling, and puts my pieces in optimal positions to exploit Black's exposed king in subsequent moves.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3"}
