 c3, e3, f4, e7, f6, b6), and puts pressure on Black's position. From d5, the knight supports potential pawn advances and coordinates better with my other pieces.\n\nReason: N: c7-d5 is the strongest move because it:\n- Centralizes my most poorly placed piece\n- Controls critical squares around Black's exposed king\n- Supports future coordination with my rook and bishop\n- Prepares potential tactical threats\n- Improves my position without committing pawns prematurely\n\nAnswer: N: c7-d5"}
